::GEO_U06_L06_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
If k \= 4, how do lengths change?
{
~A. They become 16 times as large#That is the area or surface area effect.
=B. They become 4 times as large#Linear measurements change by k.
~C. They become 64 times as large#That is the volume effect.
~D. They become 8 times as large#This doubles k instead of using k.
}
::GEO_U06_L06_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
If k \= 4, how does area change?
{
~A. 4 times#That is the length effect.
~B. 64 times#That is the volume effect.
~C. 8 times#This does not square k.
=D. 16 times#Area changes by k?, so 4?\=16.
}
::GEO_U06_L06_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
If k \= 4, how does volume change?
{
=A. 64 times#Volume changes by k?, so 4?\=64.
~B. 4 times#That is the length effect.
~C. 16 times#That is the area effect.
~D. 12 times#Multiplying by 3 is not the volume rule.
}
::GEO_U06_L06_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
A similar solid has volume scale factor 27. What is the linear scale factor?
{
~A. 27#That is the volume scale factor, not the linear scale factor.
~B. 9#This is not the cube root of 27.
=C. 3#Since 3?\=27, the linear scale factor is 3.
~D. 6#This does not match k?\=27.
}
::GEO_U06_L06_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
Which statement is true for scale factor k?
{
~A. Area changes by k and volume changes by k?#This shifts each exponent too low.
=B. Area changes by k? and volume changes by k?#Area is two-dimensional and volume is three-dimensional.
~C. All measurements change by k#Only linear measurements change by k.
~D. Volume changes by k?#Volume changes by k?.
}
